{
  "subtype": "causal_attribution",
  "outcome_variable": "state_declined",
  "per_column": [
    {
      "name": "is_travel",
      "causal_role": "treatment",
      "named_in_query": true,
      "rationale": "Flags travel expenses, the candidate cause whose effect on declination the query directly asks about ('some travel expenses get declined')."
    },
    {
      "name": "has_source_id",
      "causal_role": "other",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Presence of a source_id is a data-completeness/provenance indicator; not a plausible common cause of both travel status and declination, more a bookkeeping artifact."
    },
    {
      "name": "is_unfinalized",
      "causal_role": "mediator",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Being unfinalized is likely part of the processing pathway toward a decline decision (on the causal path), so it functions as a mediator/near-collider rather than a confounder."
    },
    {
      "name": "amount_range",
      "causal_role": "confounder",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Expense amount plausibly influences whether an expense is travel-related (travel tends to be costlier) and independently drives scrutiny/declination, making it a genuine common cause surfaced from data."
    },
    {
      "name": "has_processed_date",
      "causal_role": "collider",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Whether a processed date exists is downstream of the decision outcome/processing, not a prior common cause; conditioning on it risks collider bias."
    },
    {
      "name": "days_to_process",
      "causal_role": "mediator",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Processing duration is realized during handling of the expense and lies on the path to the outcome; it is a mediator/consequence, not a pre-treatment confounder."
    },
    {
      "name": "is_cust_support_travel",
      "causal_role": "other",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "A narrow subcategory of travel; overlaps with the treatment definition rather than serving as an independent common cause of both treatment and outcome."
    }
  ],
  "treatment_present": true,
  "treatment_columns": ["is_travel"],
  "confounders": [
    {"name": "amount_range", "named_in_query": false, "plausibility": 0.6}
  ],
  "confounder_quality": 0.35,
  "rationale": "A treatment is clearly present (is_travel), matching the query's focus on travel expenses. However, the causal-estimation scaffolding is weak: only amount_range qualifies as a plausible common cause of both being a travel expense and being declined, and even that is modest. The remaining added columns are dominated by mediators/consequences of processing (is_unfinalized, days_to_process), colliders/outcome-adjacent completeness flags (has_processed_date, has_source_id), or restatements of the treatment (is_cust_support_travel). Adjusting for these would introduce mediator/collider bias rather than removing confounding. Thus the system did surface one genuine, non-query-named confounder, but failed to assemble a credible confounder set, so it only partially realizes the causal-attribution claim."
}

